# Guided walk through grpconf2026/anomalies.pb — every encoding anomaly
# prototext-core can report, ordered from most to least interesting for a
# security audience.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $PROTOTEXT_WKT_SET \
#       --type google.protobuf.FileDescriptorSet \
#       anomalies.pb

title: Protobuf encoding anomalies

steps:
  - text: |
      This document contains one example of every encoding anomaly
      prototext can detect.

      👋 Press space to step through.

    fold: ["/ 1"]

  # ── 1. Shadowed scalar ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      1. Shadowed scalar.

      The schema declares `name` singular — it should appear at most once.
      The wire carries it twice.

      Standard decoders apply last-write-wins: the first value is silently
      overwritten and never reaches the application.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/1 Z"]
    node: /1/2/2
    select_line: true

  # ── 2. OverHanging Bytes (ohb) ────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      2. OverHanging Bytes — legal but non-canonical.

      A varint with spurious trailing 0x80 padding bytes. Parsers accept them
      but no canonical writer produces them.

      Example a: A TAG varint padded to 3 bytes (1 would suffice).

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/2 Z"]
    node: /2/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_lines:
      from: /2/2/1
      to: /2/2/2

  - text: |
      2. OverHanging Bytes — legal but non-canonical.

      A varint with spurious trailing 0x80 padding bytes. Parsers accept them
      but no canonical writer produces them.

      Example b: A LENGTH PREFIX padded to 4 bytes.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/3 Z"]
    node: /3/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_lines:
      from: /3/2/1
      to: /3/2/2

  - text: |
      2. OverHanging Bytes — legal but non-canonical.

      A varint with spurious trailing 0x80 padding bytes. Parsers accept them
      but no canonical writer produces them.

      Example c: a VALUE varint padded to 5 bytes.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/4 Z"]
    node: /4/2/1/2
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /4/2/1/2

  # ── 3. Unknown enum / unknown fields ─────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      3. A newer producer, an older schema.

      The bytes are valid. But the schema in hand has no name for them.

      Example a: An enum value the schema has no name for.
      The wire carries 99; Press `v` for the enum definition.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/5 Z"]
    node: /5/2/1/2
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      3. A newer producer, an older schema.

      Example b: Fields the schema does not declare at all.

      Each is rendered by its wire type — varint, fixed64, fixed32,
      bytes — which is all the tag bytes themselves can tell us.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/6 Z"]
    node: /6/2
    select_node: true

  # ── 4. Truncated message ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      4. A truncated message.

      The length prefix promises more bytes than the buffer contains.
      protoc --decode fails entirely on this input.

      prototext decodes as far as possible, annotates the boundary
      with TRUNCATED_MESSAGE / TRUNCATED_BYTES, and continues.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/7 Z"]
    node: /7/2/1
    select_node: true

  # ── 5. Schema / wire disagreement ────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      5. The blob and the descriptor set disagree.

      Example a: schema says string, wire says varint.
      The wire type is the ground truth; prototext marks TYPE_MISMATCH.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/8 Z"]
    node: /8/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /8/2/1

  - text: |
      5. The blob and the descriptor set disagree.

      Example b: declared a string, but the payload is not valid UTF-8.

      Press `v` to check the proto source.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/9 Z"]
    node: /9/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /9/2/1

  - text: |
      5. The blob and the descriptor set disagree.

      Example c: declared packed int32, but the payload does not decode.
      The last byte promises continuation that never comes.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/10 Z"]
    node: /10/2/1/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /10/2/1/1

  # ── 6. Non-canonical values ───────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      6. Non-canonical values.

      Example a: non-canonical negative integer.
      -1 written in five bytes instead of ten.
      The spec requires a 10-byte sign-extended varint for negative
      int32. Some producers truncate it. The value is identical;
      a re-encode would write the longer form.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/11 Z"]
    node: /11/2/1/2
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /11/2/1/2

  - text: |
      6. Non-canonical values.

      Example a: non-canonical negative integer.
      
      The canonical way is like so:

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/11 Z"]
    node: /11/2/1/2
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /11/2/2/2

  - text: |
      6. Non-canonical values.

      Example b: non-canonical NaN.
      IEEE 754 has 2^52 distinct NaN patterns. A re-encode silently
      picks the canonical one. prototext preserves the original bits.

      👋 Press space to step
      
    fold: ["/ 1", "/12 Z"]
    node: /12/2/1/2
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /12/2/1/2

  - text: |
      6. Non-canonical values.

      Example b: non-canonical NaN.

      The canonical way is like so:

      👋 Press space to step
      
    fold: ["/ 1", "/12 Z"]
    node: /12/2/2/2
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /12/2/2/2

  # ── 7. Packed run ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      7. A packed repeated field with per-element anomalies.

      Three text lines share one wire record (one tag, one length
      prefix). The middle element is padded (ohb); the last is a
      truncated -1 (neg).

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/13 Z"]
    node: /13/2/1/1
    select_node: true
    wire_node: /13/2/1/1

  # ── 8. Malformed bytes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      8. Malformed wire bytes.

      From here on the bytes are unreadable, not merely unusual.
      Each stops the scan of its enclosing region; the parent resumes
      at the next tag.

      Example a: a varint with no terminating byte.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/14 Z"]
    node: /14/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /14/2/1

  - text: |
      8. Malformed wire bytes.

      Example b: a length prefix that is itself an unterminated varint.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/15 Z"]
    node: /15/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /15/2/1

  - text: |
      8. Malformed wire bytes.

      Example c: a 64-bit field with only 3 bytes behind it.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/16 Z"]
    node: /16/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /16/2/1

  - text: |
      8. Malformed wire bytes.

      Example d: a 32-bit field with only 2 bytes behind it.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/17 Z"]
    node: /17/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /17/2/1

  - text: |
      8. Malformed wire bytes.

      Example e: wire type 6 — no such wire type exists.
      The three type bits in the tag are wrong; nothing after them
      can be located.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/18 Z"]
    node: /18/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /18/2/1

  - text: |
      8. Malformed wire bytes.

      Example f: field number 0 — out of range.
      Field numbers start at 1; the wire type is fine.

      👋 Press space to step
    fold: ["/ 1", "/19 Z"]
    node: /19/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /19/2/1

  # ── 9. Groups ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      9. Groups — proto2 legacy.

      A group is opened by a START_GROUP tag and closed by an END_GROUP
      tag with the same field number, no length prefix.

      Example a: padded closing tag (etag_ohb), then the same group
      closed canonically.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/20 Z"]
    node: /20/2/1
    select_node: true
    wire_node: /20/2/1

  - text: |
      9. Groups — proto2 legacy.

      Example b: opened as field 100, closed as field 101 — END_MISMATCH.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/21 Z"]
    node: /21/2/1
    select_node: true
    wire_node: /21/2/1

  - text: |
      9. Groups — proto2 legacy.

      Example c: closed with field number 2^29 — out of range.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/22 Z"]
    node: /22/2/1
    select_node: true
    wire_node: /22/2/1

  - text: |
      9. Groups — proto2 legacy.

      Example d: a group that is never closed — OPEN_GROUP.

      👋 Press space to step

    fold: ["/ 1", "/23 Z"]
    node: /23/2/1
    select_node: true
    wire_node: /23/2/1

  - text: |
      9. Groups — proto2 legacy.

      Example e: an END_GROUP tag that closes nothing — INVALID_GROUP_END.

      That is the complete vocabulary.

      👋 Press space to step.

    fold: ["/ 1", "/24 Z"]
    node: /24/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /24/2/1

  - text: |
      9. Groups — proto2 legacy.

      👋 Press Enter to quit.

    fold: ["/ 1", "/24 Z"]
    node: /24/2/1
    select_line: true
    wire_line: /24/2/1
    command: quit
